prompt:
A codebase has the same email-format check copied into three classes:

```java
// UserRegistrationService
if (!email.matches("^[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}$")) { reject(); }

// NewsletterSignupController
if (!email.matches("^[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}$")) { reject(); }

// AdminUserController
if (!email.matches("^[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}$")) { reject(); }
```

A bug report shows that when the compliance team tightened the rule to reject `+` aliases, someone updated the regex only in `UserRegistrationService`. Newsletter signups still accept addresses that registration now rejects. What is the best way to apply DRY here?
